[[:db/cas 17592186663428 :question/problem "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=z+5$, the section in the plane $4x+\\sqrt{3}y=8$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto the $xz$-plane." "For the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=z+5,\n$$ the section in the plane $4x+\\sqrt{3}y=8$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto the $xz$-plane."] [:db/cas 17592186664045 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x-9y+z \\\\ 2z-6x+10y \\\\ -3y+x-z\\end{bmatrix},$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$" "Given the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x-9y+z \\\\ 2z-6x+10y \\\\ -3y+x-z\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186664062 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{3}^{x+1}}{{3}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{3}^{x+1}}{{3}^{x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186664068 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x-9y+3z \\\\ 2x-5y+2z \\\\ 6x-4y+6z\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$" "Consider the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3x-9y+3z \\\\ 2x-5y+2z \\\\ 6x-4y+6z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186664097 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation \n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y-z \\\\ -2y+z \\\\ 2x+4y-3z\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand the vectors \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$" "Consider the linear transformation \n$$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+y-z \\\\ -2y+z \\\\ 2x+4y-3z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nand the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$"] [:db/cas 17592186664772 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186664781 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 3 & 2 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186664783 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.91 & -0.58 \\\\ 0.51 & 0.18 \\\\ 1.16 & 1.33 \\\\ -0.75 & -0.93\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.435 & 0.626 & 0.554 & -0.335 \\\\ 0.202 & -0.625 & 0.562 & -0.502 \\\\ 0.727 & 0.319 & 0.454 & 0.403 \\\\ -0.491 & -0.339 & 0.413 & 0.688\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.421 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.374 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $\\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.91 & -0.58 \\\\ 0.51 & 0.18 \\\\ 1.16 & 1.33 \\\\ -0.75 & -0.93\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.435 \\\\ 0.202 \\\\ 0.727 \\\\ -0.491\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2.421$" "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.91 & -0.58 \\\\ 0.51 & 0.18 \\\\ 1.16 & 1.33 \\\\ -0.75 & -0.93\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.435 & 0.626 & 0.554 & -0.335 \\\\ 0.202 & -0.625 & 0.562 & -0.502 \\\\ 0.727 & 0.319 & 0.454 & 0.403 \\\\ -0.491 & -0.339 & 0.413 & 0.688\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}2.421 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0.374 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.707 & -0.707 \\\\ 0.707 & 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $\\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-0.91 & -0.58 \\\\ 0.51 & 0.18 \\\\ 1.16 & 1.33 \\\\ -0.75 & -0.93\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.707 \\\\ 0.707\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIII. $\\begin{bmatrix}-0.435 \\\\ 0.202 \\\\ 0.727 \\\\ -0.491\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2.421$"] [:db/cas 17592186664794 :question/problem "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.490 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $\\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $\\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}$" "Suppose we have a dataset consisting of $4$ datapoints with $2$ features in the form of the $4\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere the data has been standardized (the mean of each feature is $0$ and the standard deviation is $1$).\nWe are also given the SVD decomposition $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ where:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\begin{bmatrix}-0.583 & -0.434 & 0.687 & 0.016 \\\\ 0.490 & -0.561 & 0.046 & 0.666 \\\\ 0.506 & 0.466 & 0.725 & -0.029 \\\\ -0.405 & 0.529 & -0.027 & 0.746\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ Σ & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.917 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1.528 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ V & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.709 & 0.706 \\\\ -0.706 & 0.709\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following matrices corresponds to the one-dimensional version of the data that preserves the most variance?\n\nI. $\\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nII. $\\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}-1.26 & 0.32 \\\\ 0.06 & -1.27 \\\\ 1.19 & -0.18 \\\\ 0.02 & 1.12\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0.709 \\\\ -0.706\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186664813 :question/problem "Simplify the product of permutations ${\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 & 1\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.$" "Simplify the product of permutations $$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 4 & 1\\end{pmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186664831 :question/problem "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{2x-4}{3}|+\\frac{1}{6}<\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" "Solve the absolute value inequality $$\n\\displaystyle |\\frac{2x-4}{3}|+\\frac{1}{6}<\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186665361 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ does lie in $\\text{Ker}(T),$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ does lie in $\\text{Ker}(T),$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\}.$ Therefore, according to the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne \\{0\\},$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186665379 :question/problem "Find ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(8{n}^{2}+3n+4)$ in terms of $N.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(8{n}^{2}+3n+4)\n$$ in terms of $N.$"] [:db/cas 17592186665379 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(8{n}^{2}+3n+4) & =8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+4N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[8(N+1)(2N+1)+9(N+1)+24] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[16{N}^{2}+24N+8+9N+9+24] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[16{N}^{2}+33N+41]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(8{n}^{2}+3n+4) & =8{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+4N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[8(N+1)(2N+1)+9(N+1)+24] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[16{N}^{2}+24N+8+9N+9+24] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[16{N}^{2}+33N+41]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186665383 :question/problem "$\\int {x}^{2}({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186665383 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{5}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27{ln}^{2}⁡5}-\\frac{2D}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6{log}_{5}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡5})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$" "We need to use integration by parts.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{5}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}{log}_{5}⁡x}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{27{ln}^{2}⁡5}-\\frac{2D}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{27}(9{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6{log}_{5}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡5})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{2D}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186665384 :question/problem "Calculate\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}6{n}^{2}.$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{20}6{n}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186665386 :question/problem "Calculate\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n(7-2n).$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n(7-2n).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186665386 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n(7-2n) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}(-2{n}^{2}+7n) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}(-2){n}^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}7n \\\\  & =-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}{n}^{2}+7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately. For the first series, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}{n}^{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18(18+1)(2\\cdot 18+1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18\\cdot 19\\cdot 37 \\\\  & =4\\,218.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n & =7(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 18(18+1)) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 171 \\\\  & =1\\,197.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n(7-2n) & =-4\\,218+1\\,197 \\\\  & =-\\,3\\,021.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\nWe can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n(7-2n) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}(-2{n}^{2}+7n) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}(-2){n}^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}7n \\\\  & =-2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}{n}^{2}+7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the two series separately. For the first series, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}{n}^{2} & =2(\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18(18+1)(2\\cdot 18+1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18\\cdot 19\\cdot 37 \\\\  & =4\\,218.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second series, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n & =7(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 18(18+1)) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 171 \\\\  & =1\\,197.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{18}n(7-2n) & =-4\\,218+1\\,197 \\\\  & =-\\,3\\,021.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186665399 :question/problem "$T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" "$T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\bbox[3pt,white,border:2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{WW}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?"] [:db/cas 17592186665399 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $\\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{nullity}(T).$" "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* (in terms of linear transformations) states the following: \n\n $T$ is a bijection $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$ \n$\\,⟺\\,$  $T$ is invertible\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\text{nullity}(T).$"] [:db/cas 17592186665407 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡(5)}(${{field-1}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡(5)}(\n$${{field-1}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186665407 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=8x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 8x\\,\\text{d}x=4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}(4{x}^{2})-\\int (4{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int x{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int x{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{5}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{x}^{2}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{8D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{5}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡e-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡1-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4-4+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(0-0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(\\,2{e}^{2}-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=8x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 8x\\,\\text{d}x=4{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}(4{x}^{2})-\\int (4{x}^{2})\\left(\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int x{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{5}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{5}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{5}⁡x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{x}^{2}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+D) \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}{({log}_{5}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}{log}_{5}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}-\\frac{4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{8D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{5}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}8x({log}_{5}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡x-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡e-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4{ln}^{2}⁡1-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(4-4+2)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(0-0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}-\\frac{2}{{ln}^{2}⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡5}(\\,2{e}^{2}-2\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186665419 :question/problem "Calculate\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{32}3{n}^{2}.$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{32}3{n}^{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186665428 :question/problem "Find ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+3)$ in terms of $N.$" "Find $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+3)\n$$ in terms of $N.$"] [:db/cas 17592186665428 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+3) & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+3N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[5(N+1)(2N+1)+9(N+1)+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+15N+5+9N+9+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+24N+32] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{3}[5{N}^{2}+12N+16]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, let's recall the following formulas:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n=\\frac{1}{2}N(N+1)\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}=\\frac{1}{6}N(N+1)(2N+1)\n$$\n\nUsing these results, we can write the given summation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}(5{n}^{2}+3n+3) & =5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{n}^{2}+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}n+3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}1 \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{N}{6}(N+1)(2N+1)+3\\cdot \\frac{N}{2}(N+1)+3N \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[5(N+1)(2N+1)+9(N+1)+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+15N+5+9N+9+18] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{6}[10{N}^{2}+24N+32] \\\\  & =\\frac{N}{3}[5{N}^{2}+12N+16]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666060 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$" "Evaluate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+2}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}} $-\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(${{field-2}}$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186666062 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2(4) \\\\ 2(6) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 168 \\\\ -40 & 136\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{y}_{1} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2(4) \\\\ 2(6) & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 24 \\\\ 24 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 168 \\\\ -40 & 136\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666063 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨4,6,5⟩)\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩=0\\,$ and $\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3 \\\\ y &=-4-2t \\\\ z &=3-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨4,6,5⟩)\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩=0\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-3 \\\\ y &=-4-2t \\\\ z &=3-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666068 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x-3)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666069 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${g}_{14}$ in \n$G=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Find the cofactor of ${g}_{14}$ in \n$$\n\\displaystyle G=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666071 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $r=⟨-7,24,-28⟩+s⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\,$ and $\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+4t \\\\ y &=4-2t \\\\ z &=4-6t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $r=⟨-7,24,-28⟩+s⟨2,-1,-3⟩\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3+4t \\\\ y &=4-2t \\\\ z &=4-6t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666072 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨1,1,5⟩)\\times ⟨5,-4,2⟩=0\\,$ and $\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-5-5t \\\\ y &=4+4t \\\\ z &=-19-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.$" "Find the shortest distance between the lines ${l}_{1}$ and ${l}_{2}$ with equations\n\n$\\,$ $(r-⟨1,1,5⟩)\\times ⟨5,-4,2⟩=0\\,$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-5-5t \\\\ y &=4+4t \\\\ z &=-19-2t\\end{aligned}\\right.,\\,t\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666073 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186666073 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2(0)^{2} \\\\ {0}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4{x}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{x}_{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{2}^{3}-{y}_{1})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}_{1} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3{y}_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}1+2(0)^{2} \\\\ {0}^{2}-1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4{x}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2{x}_{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666075 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax &=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 4{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-1-4{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-1-4(-1)+5(1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax &=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 4{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =2+{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-1-4{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-1-4(-1)+5(1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186666088 :question/problem "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined by\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}.\\,f(y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWhat is \n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at \n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td a