th=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$128$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$128$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186667664 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$36$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$72$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$180$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$36$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$72$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$180$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186667671 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?$" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=3({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186667674 :question/problem "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1.$" "Find the asymptotes of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186667677 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-3b)^{T}(2Ax-3b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-160$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-64$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-3b)^{T}(2Ax-3b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-160$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-64$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186670019 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{10}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{15}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=3\\sqrt{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $|\\,a\\,|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}|=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.$" "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: We set ${v}_{2}={a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find ${v}_{3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{3}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{10}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{15}{15}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we normalize ${v}_{3}$ dividing it by $‖{v}_{3}‖=3\\sqrt{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3} & =\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle |\\,a\\,|=|-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}|=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670543 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{x})$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{x})\n$$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186670543 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to make sure that the calculator is in $\\,\\text{RAD}\\,$ (radians) mode.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{x})|}_{x=\\pi /2}≈4.810\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, we need to make sure that the calculator is in $\\,\\text{RAD}\\,$ (radians) mode.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{x})|}_{x=\\pi /2}≈4.810\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186670544 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(4)={5}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+1)/4}≈2.612\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(4)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)/x})|}_{x=4}≈-0.364\\,2\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.364\\,2$ and the coordinates of our point $(4,2.612\\,1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2.612\\,1 & =-0.364\\,2(x-4) \\\\ y-2.612\\,1 & =-0.364\\,2x+1.456\\,8 \\\\ y & =-0.364\\,2x+4.0689.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.4x+4.1\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(4)={5}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+1)/4}≈2.612\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=4.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(4)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)/x})|}_{x=4}≈-0.364\\,2\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.364\\,2$ and the coordinates of our point $(4,2.612\\,1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2.612\\,1 & =-0.364\\,2(x-4) \\\\ y-2.612\\,1 & =-0.364\\,2x+1.456\\,8 \\\\ y & =-0.364\\,2x+4.0689.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.4x+4.1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670545 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=0.6$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(0.6)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.6))^{1-0.6}≈0.795\\,6\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=0.6.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(0.6)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1-x})|}_{x=0.6}≈0.919\\,9\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=0.919\\,9$ and the coordinates of our point $(0.6,0.795\\,6),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.795\\,6 & =0.919\\,9(x-0.6) \\\\ y-0.795\\,6 & =0.919\\,9x-0.551\\,9 \\\\ y & =0.919\\,9x+0.243\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=0.9x+0.2\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=0.6$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(0.6)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.6))^{1-0.6}≈0.795\\,6\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=0.6.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(0.6)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{1-x})|}_{x=0.6}≈0.919\\,9\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=0.919\\,9$ and the coordinates of our point $(0.6,0.795\\,6),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.795\\,6 & =0.919\\,9(x-0.6) \\\\ y-0.795\\,6 & =0.919\\,9x-0.551\\,9 \\\\ y & =0.919\\,9x+0.243\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=0.9x+0.2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670546 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(-1)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}))≈0.570\\,8\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})))|}_{x=-1}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=2$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,0.570\\,8),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.570\\,8 & =2(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-0.570\\,8 & =2(x+1) \\\\ y-0.570\\,8 & =2x+2 \\\\ y & =2x+2.570\\,8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=2.0x+2.6\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(-1)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}))≈0.570\\,8\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})))|}_{x=-1}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=2$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,0.570\\,8),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.570\\,8 & =2(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-0.570\\,8 & =2(x+1) \\\\ y-0.570\\,8 & =2x+2 \\\\ y & =2x+2.570\\,8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=2.0x+2.6\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670547 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}{x-1}\\right)$ at $x=3.6.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}{x-1}\\right)\n$$ at $x=3.6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186670547 :question/worked-solution "We bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\left(\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}{x-1}\\right)|}_{x=3.6}≈0.195\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "We bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\left(\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|}{x-1}\\right)|}_{x=3.6}≈0.195\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186670548 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=0.5$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(0.5)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2(0.5)\\sqrt{1-(0.5)^{2}})≈1.047\\,2\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places. \n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=0.5.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(0.5)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})|}_{x=0.5}≈2.309\\,4\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0.5)}=-\\frac{1}{2.309\\,4}=-0.433\\,0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.433\\,0$ and the coordinates of our point $(0.5,1.047\\,2),$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1.047\\,2 & =-0.433\\,0(x-0.5) \\\\ y-1.047\\,2 & =-0.433\\,0x+0.216\\,5 \\\\ y & =-0.433\\,0x+1.263\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.4x+1.3.\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=0.5$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(0.5)=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2(0.5)\\sqrt{1-(0.5)^{2}})≈1.047\\,2\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places. \n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=0.5.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(0.5)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}})|}_{x=0.5}≈2.309\\,4\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0.5)}=-\\frac{1}{2.309\\,4}=-0.433\\,0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.433\\,0$ and the coordinates of our point $(0.5,1.047\\,2),$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1.047\\,2 & =-0.433\\,0(x-0.5) \\\\ y-1.047\\,2 & =-0.433\\,0x+0.216\\,5 \\\\ y & =-0.433\\,0x+1.263\\,7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.4x+1.3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670549 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to make sure that the calculator is in $\\,\\text{RAD}\\,$ (radians) mode.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(({x}^{3}-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x}))|}_{x=2}≈-34.70\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "First, we need to make sure that the calculator is in $\\,\\text{RAD}\\,$ (radians) mode.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(({x}^{3}-x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{x}))|}_{x=2}≈-34.70\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186670550 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2}+2)}{{2}^{3}+1}≈0.199\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{3}+1}\\right)|}_{x=2}≈-0.191\\,4\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}=-\\frac{1}{-0.191\\,4}=5.224\\,7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=5.224\\,6$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,0.199\\,1),$ and get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.199\\,1 & =5.224\\,7(x-2) \\\\ y-0.199\\,1 & =5.227\\,6x-10.449\\,4 \\\\ y & =5.224\\,7x-10.250\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=5.2x-10.3.\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2}+2)}{{2}^{3}+1}≈0.199\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)}{{x}^{3}+1}\\right)|}_{x=2}≈-0.191\\,4\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}=-\\frac{1}{-0.191\\,4}=5.224\\,7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=5.224\\,6$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,0.199\\,1),$ and get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.199\\,1 & =5.224\\,7(x-2) \\\\ y-0.199\\,1 & =5.227\\,6x-10.449\\,4 \\\\ y & =5.224\\,7x-10.250\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=5.2x-10.3.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670551 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=1$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+(1)^{3}}}{1+{2}^{(1)-1}}≈0.707\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{3}}}{1+{2}^{x-1}}\\right)|}_{x=1}≈0.285\\,3\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)}=-\\frac{1}{0.285\\,3}=-3.505\\,1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-3.505\\,5$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,0.707\\,1),$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.707\\,1 & =-3.505\\,1(x-1) \\\\ y-0.707\\,1 & =-3.505\\,1x+3.505\\,1 \\\\ y & =-3.505\\,1x+4.212\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-3.5x+4.2.\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=1$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{1+(1)^{3}}}{1+{2}^{(1)-1}}≈0.707\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(1)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{3}}}{1+{2}^{x-1}}\\right)|}_{x=1}≈0.285\\,3\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)}=-\\frac{1}{0.285\\,3}=-3.505\\,1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-3.505\\,5$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,0.707\\,1),$ and get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0.707\\,1 & =-3.505\\,1(x-1) \\\\ y-0.707\\,1 & =-3.505\\,1x+3.505\\,1 \\\\ y & =-3.505\\,1x+4.212\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-3.5x+4.2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670552 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{1+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}}≈1.949\\,4\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}})|}_{x=-\\pi /3}≈-0.853\\,0.\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.853\\,0$ and the coordinates of our point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},1.949\\,4)≈(-1.047\\,2,1.949\\,4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1.949\\,4 & =-0.853\\,0(x-(-1.047\\,2)) \\\\ y-1.949\\,4 & =-0.853\\,0(x+1.047\\,2) \\\\ y-1.949\\,4 & =-0.853\\,0x-0.893\\,3 \\\\ y & =-0.853\\,0x+1.056\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.9x+1.1\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{1+{cos}^{2}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}}≈1.949\\,4\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{\\frac{4+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1+{cos}^{2}⁡x}})|}_{x=-\\pi /3}≈-0.853\\,0.\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.853\\,0$ and the coordinates of our point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},1.949\\,4)≈(-1.047\\,2,1.949\\,4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1.949\\,4 & =-0.853\\,0(x-(-1.047\\,2)) \\\\ y-1.949\\,4 & =-0.853\\,0(x+1.047\\,2) \\\\ y-1.949\\,4 & =-0.853\\,0x-0.893\\,3 \\\\ y & =-0.853\\,0x+1.056\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate tangent line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.9x+1.1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670553 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+3}{2x-1}\\right))$ at $x=\\pi .$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+3}{2x-1}\\right))\n$$ at $x=\\pi .$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186670553 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to make sure that the calculator is in $\\,\\text{RAD}\\,$ (radians) mode.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+3}{2x-1}\\right))|}_{x=\\pi }≈-0.10\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." "First, we need to make sure that the calculator is in $\\,\\text{RAD}\\,$ (radians) mode.\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating this using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{x+3}{2x-1}\\right))|}_{x=\\pi }≈-0.10\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186670554 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(-1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1)+2})≈1.010\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}))|}_{x=-1}≈0.074\\,6\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{0.074\\,6}=-13.404\\,8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-13.404\\,8$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,1.010\\,5),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1.010\\,5 & =-13.404\\,8(x+1) \\\\ y-1.010\\,5 & =-13.404\\,8x-13.404\\,8 \\\\ y & =-13.404\\,8x-12.394\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-13.4x-12.4.\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=-1$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(-1)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-1)+2})≈1.010\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=-1.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(-1)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}))|}_{x=-1}≈0.074\\,6\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)}=-\\frac{1}{0.074\\,6}=-13.404\\,8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-13.404\\,8$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,1.010\\,5),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1.010\\,5 & =-13.404\\,8(x+1) \\\\ y-1.010\\,5 & =-13.404\\,8x-13.404\\,8 \\\\ y & =-13.404\\,8x-12.394\\,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-13.4x-12.4.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670555 :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(2)=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2}\\cdot {e}^{2}≈7.158\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $\\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{x})|}_{x=2}≈6.066\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}=-\\frac{1}{6.066\\,5}=-0.164\\,8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.164\\,8$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,7.158\\,5),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-7.158\\,5 & =-0.164\\,8(x-2) \\\\ y-7.158\\,5 & =-0.164\\,8x+0.329\\,6 \\\\ y & =-0.164\\,8x+7.488\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.2x+7.5.\n$$" "Evaluating $f(x)$ at the point $x=2$ using a calculator, we get\n\n$$\ny=f(2)=\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2}\\cdot {e}^{2}≈7.158\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nThe slope of the normal line is the negative reciprocal of the derivative of $f(x)$ at $x=2.$\n\nWe bring up the derivative option on most calculators in one of the following ways:\n\n- Using the $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[2px,border:2px solid gray]{\\color{gray}{\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\bbox[gray]{\\phantom{00}}\\,}}\n$$ button. You may need to press the $\\,2\\text{nd}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{shift}\\,$) button first.\n\n- Press the $\\,\\text{math}\\,$ (or $\\,\\text{calc}\\,$) button and choose the derivative option. Note that this might be called $\\,\\text{nDeriv}\\,$ or similar.\n\nEvaluating the derivative using a calculator, we obtain\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)={\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {e}^{x})|}_{x=2}≈6.066\\,5\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the normal is\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)}=-\\frac{1}{6.066\\,5}=-0.164\\,8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-0.164\\,8$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,7.158\\,5),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-7.158\\,5 & =-0.164\\,8(x-2) \\\\ y-7.158\\,5 & =-0.164\\,8x+0.329\\,6 \\\\ y & =-0.164\\,8x+7.488\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding the coefficients to one decimal place, we find that the approximate normal line is given by\n\n$$\ny=-0.2x+7.5.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670673 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(-2)y}{4}?$" "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(-2)y}{4}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670691 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(2)y}{3}?$" "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(2)y}{3}?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670787 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{2}}{z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{2}}{z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$." "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{2}}{z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)+\\log_{5}\\left(y^{2}\\right)-\\log_{5}\\left(z^{4}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log_{5}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{2}}{z^{4}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2}\\log_{5}\\left(x\\right)+2\\log_{5}\\left(y\\right)-4\\log_{5}\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186670788 :question/worked-solution "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{3}}{z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\log\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)-5\\log\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{3}}{z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\log\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)-5\\log\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$." "The laws of logarithms are:\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)+\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(ab\\right)$ — the product rule\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\log_{n}\\left(a\\right)-\\log_{n}\\left(b\\right)=\\log_{n}\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$ — the quotient rule\n\n- $\\log_{n}\\left(c^{m}\\right)=m\\log_{n}\\left(c\\right)$ — the power rule\n\nUsing the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{3}}{z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the product rule for logarithms, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}y^{3}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power rule for logarithms (a square root is a power of one half), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(y^{3}\\right)+\\log\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-\\log\\left(z^{5}\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\log\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)-5\\log\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\log\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{x}y^{3}}{z^{5}}\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\log\\left(y\\right)+\\frac{1}{2}\\log\\left(x\\right)-5\\log\\left(z\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese identities hold for $x,y,z>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186670923 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{2+3x}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[4]{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3x&=\\ln\\left(3^{1/4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3x&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(3\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(3\\right)-2 \\\\ x&=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(3\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(3\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{2+3x}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[4]{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3x&=\\ln\\left(3^{1/4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+3x&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(3\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(3\\right)-2 \\\\ x&=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(3\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(3\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670925 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{5y+2}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[3]{13}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y+2&=\\ln\\left(13^{1/3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y+2&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(13\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(13\\right)-2 \\\\ y&=-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(13\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(13\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{5y+2}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[3]{13}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y+2&=\\ln\\left(13^{1/3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y+2&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(13\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(13\\right)-2 \\\\ y&=-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(13\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle y=-\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(13\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670926 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-7-2x}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[8]{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-2x&=\\ln\\left(7^{1/8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-2x&=\\frac{1}{8}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x&=\\frac{1}{8}\\ln\\left(7\\right)+7 \\\\ x&=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\\ln\\left(7\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-7-2x}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[8]{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-2x&=\\ln\\left(7^{1/8}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-2x&=\\frac{1}{8}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x&=\\frac{1}{8}\\ln\\left(7\\right)+7 \\\\ x&=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle x=-\\frac{7}{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\\ln\\left(7\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670928 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-3x+4}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[4]{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4&=\\ln\\left(7^{1/4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(7\\right)-4 \\\\ x&=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(7\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-3x+4}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[4]{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4&=\\ln\\left(7^{1/4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+4&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(7\\right)-4 \\\\ x&=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{4}{3}-\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(7\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670929 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-3t+8}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[5]{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3t+8&=\\ln\\left(7^{1/5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3t+8&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3t&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(7\\right)-8 \\\\ t&=\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t=\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(7\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-3t+8}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[5]{7}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3t+8&=\\ln\\left(7^{1/5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3t+8&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3t&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(7\\right)-8 \\\\ t&=\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(7\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{8}{3}-\\frac{1}{15}\\ln\\left(7\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670930 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-2+5t}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[4]{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+5t&=\\ln\\left(2^{1/4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+5t&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(2\\right)+2 \\\\ t&=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(2\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-2+5t}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[4]{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+5t&=\\ln\\left(2^{1/4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+5t&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5t&=\\frac{1}{4}\\ln\\left(2\\right)+2 \\\\ t&=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(2\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle t=\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(2\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670932 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-1+4x}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[3]{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+4x&=\\ln\\left(5^{1/3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+4x&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(5\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(5\\right)+1 \\\\ x&=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(5\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(5\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-1+4x}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[3]{5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+4x&=\\ln\\left(5^{1/3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1+4x&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(5\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x&=\\frac{1}{3}\\ln\\left(5\\right)+1 \\\\ x&=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(5\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{12}\\ln\\left(5\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670933 :question/worked-solution "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-4t-7}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[5]{11}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4t-7&=\\ln\\left(11^{1/5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4t-7&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(11\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4t&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(11\\right)+7 \\\\ t&=-\\frac{7}{4}-\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(11\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $t=-\\frac{7}{4}-\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(11\\right)$." "Take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\ln\\left(e^{-4t-7}\\right)&=\\ln\\left(\\sqrt[5]{11}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse $\\ln(e^u)=u$ and write the root as a fractional power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4t-7&=\\ln\\left(11^{1/5}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4t-7&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(11\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the constant term, then divide by the signed coefficient of $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4t&=\\frac{1}{5}\\ln\\left(11\\right)+7 \\\\ t&=-\\frac{7}{4}-\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(11\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle t=-\\frac{7}{4}-\\frac{1}{20}\\ln\\left(11\\right).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186670978 :question/problem "$\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{-3x^{9}-9}{x^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n\nAssume $x\\ne 0$." "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{-3x^{9}-9}{x^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n\nAssume $x\\ne 0$."] [:db/cas 17592186670986 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{