}+4x^{4}.\n$$ For real $x\\ne 0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671054 :question/problem "$\\displaystyle\\int \\left(-6+x^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\left(-6+x^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671081 :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\cdot y=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}+\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)\\cdot y=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\n$$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."] [:db/cas 17592186671089 :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\cdot y=\\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{dy}{dx}-\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)\\cdot y=\\cos{\\left(4 x \\right)}\n$$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."] [:db/cas 17592186671321 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{3y^{5}+2}{y^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Assume $y\\ne0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{3y^{5}+2}{y^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$ Assume $y\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671321 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{3y^{5}+2}{y^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(3y^{2}+\\frac{2}{y^{3}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 3\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+2\\int y^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 3\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{y^{-2}}{-2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= y^{3}-\\frac{1}{y^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{3y^{5}+2}{y^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(3y^{2}+\\frac{2}{y^{3}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 3\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+2\\int y^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 3\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{y^{-2}}{-2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= y^{3}-\\frac{1}{y^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671322 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{-y^{5}+3}{y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Assume $y\\ne0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{-y^{5}+3}{y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$ Assume $y\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671322 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{-y^{5}+3}{y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-y^{3}+\\frac{3}{y^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+3\\int y^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{y^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{y^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3}{y}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{-y^{5}+3}{y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-y^{3}+\\frac{3}{y^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+3\\int y^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{y^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{y^{4}}{4}-\\frac{3}{y}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671323 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(4-5y^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\left(4-5y^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671323 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\left(4-5y^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-40y^{2}+16+25y^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -40\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+16\\int y^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+25\\int y^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -40\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+16\\left(\\frac{y^{1}}{1}\\right)+25\\left(\\frac{y^{5}}{5}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{40y^{3}}{3}+16y+5y^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\left(4-5y^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-40y^{2}+16+25y^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -40\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+16\\int y^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+25\\int y^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -40\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+16\\left(\\frac{y^{1}}{1}\\right)+25\\left(\\frac{y^{5}}{5}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{40y^{3}}{3}+16y+5y^{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671324 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{-4t^{2}+6}{t^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t\\ne0$." "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{-4t^{2}+6}{t^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671324 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{-4t^{2}+6}{t^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-4+\\frac{6}{t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -4\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+6\\int t^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -4\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{t^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -4t-\\frac{6}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank." "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{-4t^{2}+6}{t^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-4+\\frac{6}{t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -4\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+6\\int t^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -4\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{t^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -4t-\\frac{6}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."] [:db/cas 17592186671325 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -5t-5t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int -5t-5t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671325 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -5t-5t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-5t-5t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -5\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-5\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -5\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)-5\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{5t^{2}}{2}-\\frac{5t^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -5t-5t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-5t-5t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -5\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-5\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -5\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)-5\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{5t^{2}}{2}-\\frac{5t^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671326 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -6y^{2}-6y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int -6y^{2}-6y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671326 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -6y^{2}-6y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-6y^{2}-6y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -6\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y-6\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -6\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)-6\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -2y^{3}-\\frac{3y^{4}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -6y^{2}-6y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-6y^{2}-6y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -6\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y-6\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -6\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)-6\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -2y^{3}-\\frac{3y^{4}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671327 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int x^{4}+\\sqrt[4]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $x>0$." "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int x^{4}+\\sqrt[4]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $x>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671327 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int x^{4}+\\sqrt[4]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(x^{4}+x^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int x^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int x^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{x^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{x^{5}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{x^{5}}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank." "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int x^{4}+\\sqrt[4]{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(x^{4}+x^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int x^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int x^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{x^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{x^{5}}{5}+\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{x^{5}}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."] [:db/cas 17592186671328 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int x^{3}-5\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int x^{3}-5\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671328 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int x^{3}-5\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(x^{3}-5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int x^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-5\\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)-5\\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{x^{4}}{4}-5x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int x^{3}-5\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(x^{3}-5\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int x^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-5\\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{x^{4}}{4}\\right)-5\\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{x^{4}}{4}-5x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671329 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int -\\sqrt[4]{x}-x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $x>0$." "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int -\\sqrt[4]{x}-x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $x>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671329 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -\\sqrt[4]{x}-x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-x^{\\frac{1}{4}}-x^{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -\\int x^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-\\int x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{x^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{x^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{x^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank." "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -\\sqrt[4]{x}-x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-x^{\\frac{1}{4}}-x^{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -\\int x^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-\\int x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{x^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{x^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{x^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."] [:db/cas 17592186671330 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt[5]{t^{6}}}-\\frac{4}{t^{2}}+6t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Assume $t\\ne0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt[5]{t^{6}}}-\\frac{4}{t^{2}}+6t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$ Assume $t\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671330 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt[5]{t^{6}}}-\\frac{4}{t^{2}}+6t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(\\frac{2}{t^{\\frac{6}{5}}}-\\frac{4}{t^{2}}+6t^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\int t^{-\\frac{6}{5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-4\\int t^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+6\\int t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\left(\\frac{t^{-\\frac{1}{5}}}{-\\frac{1}{5}}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{t^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{t^{5}}{5}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{10}{\\sqrt[5]{t}}+\\frac{4}{t}+\\frac{6t^{5}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{2}{\\sqrt[5]{t^{6}}}-\\frac{4}{t^{2}}+6t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(\\frac{2}{t^{\\frac{6}{5}}}-\\frac{4}{t^{2}}+6t^{4}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\int t^{-\\frac{6}{5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-4\\int t^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+6\\int t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\left(\\frac{t^{-\\frac{1}{5}}}{-\\frac{1}{5}}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{t^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{t^{5}}{5}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{10}{\\sqrt[5]{t}}+\\frac{4}{t}+\\frac{6t^{5}}{5}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671331 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -2x+3x^{2}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int -2x+3x^{2}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671331 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -2x+3x^{2}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-2x+3x^{2}-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -2\\int x^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+3\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-2\\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -2\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -x^{2}+x^{3}-2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -2x+3x^{2}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-2x+3x^{2}-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -2\\int x^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+3\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-2\\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= -2\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -x^{2}+x^{3}-2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671332 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\sqrt{t}\\left(4t-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Assume $t>0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\sqrt{t}\\left(4t-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$ Assume $t>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671332 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\sqrt{t}\\left(4t-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(4t^{\\frac{3}{2}}-2t^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\int t^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-2\\int t^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{\\frac{5}{2}}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{8\\sqrt{t^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{t^{3}}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\sqrt{t}\\left(4t-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(4t^{\\frac{3}{2}}-2t^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\int t^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-2\\int t^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{\\frac{5}{2}}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{8\\sqrt{t^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{4\\sqrt{t^{3}}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671333 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(4+t^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\left(4+t^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671333 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\left(4+t^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(8t^{2}+t^{4}+16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 8\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+16\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 8\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{t^{5}}{5}\\right)+16\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{8t^{3}}{3}+\\frac{t^{5}}{5}+16t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\left(4+t^{2}\\right)^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(8t^{2}+t^{4}+16\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 8\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+16\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 8\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{t^{5}}{5}\\right)+16\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{8t^{3}}{3}+\\frac{t^{5}}{5}+16t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671334 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6\\sqrt[5]{t^{3}}+\\frac{5}{t^{3}}+t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Assume $t\\ne0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6\\sqrt[5]{t^{3}}+\\frac{5}{t^{3}}+t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$ Assume $t\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671334 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 6\\sqrt[5]{t^{3}}+\\frac{5}{t^{3}}+t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(6t^{\\frac{3}{5}}+\\frac{5}{t^{3}}+t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 6\\int t^{\\frac{3}{5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+5\\int t^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 6\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{8}{5}}}{\\frac{8}{5}}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{t^{-2}}{-2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{15\\sqrt[5]{t^{8}}}{4}-\\frac{5}{2t^{2}}+\\frac{t^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 6\\sqrt[5]{t^{3}}+\\frac{5}{t^{3}}+t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(6t^{\\frac{3}{5}}+\\frac{5}{t^{3}}+t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 6\\int t^{\\frac{3}{5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+5\\int t^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 6\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{8}{5}}}{\\frac{8}{5}}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{t^{-2}}{-2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{15\\sqrt[5]{t^{8}}}{4}-\\frac{5}{2t^{2}}+\\frac{t^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671335 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -6\\sqrt[7]{t^{5}}-t^{5}-\\frac{4}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Assume $t\\ne0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int -6\\sqrt[7]{t^{5}}-t^{5}-\\frac{4}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$ Assume $t\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671335 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -6\\sqrt[7]{t^{5}}-t^{5}-\\frac{4}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-6t^{\\frac{5}{7}}-t^{5}-\\frac{4}{t^{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -6\\int t^{\\frac{5}{7}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-4\\int t^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -6\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{12}{7}}}{\\frac{12}{7}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{t^{-3}}{-3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{7\\sqrt[7]{t^{12}}}{2}-\\frac{t^{6}}{6}+\\frac{4}{3t^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -6\\sqrt[7]{t^{5}}-t^{5}-\\frac{4}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-6t^{\\frac{5}{7}}-t^{5}-\\frac{4}{t^{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -6\\int t^{\\frac{5}{7}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-4\\int t^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -6\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{12}{7}}}{\\frac{12}{7}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{t^{-3}}{-3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{7\\sqrt[7]{t^{12}}}{2}-\\frac{t^{6}}{6}+\\frac{4}{3t^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671336 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 5y^{3}-4y\\,\\mathrm{d}y$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int 5y^{3}-4y\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671336 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 5y^{3}-4y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(5y^{3}-4y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 5\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y-4\\int y^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 5\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{5y^{4}}{4}-2y^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 5y^{3}-4y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(5y^{3}-4y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 5\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y-4\\int y^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 5\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)-4\\left(\\frac{y^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{5y^{4}}{4}-2y^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671337 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -3+5t^{2}+4t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int -3+5t^{2}+4t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671337 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -3+5t^{2}+4t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-3+5t^{2}+4t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+5\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+4\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -3t+\\frac{5t^{3}}{3}+2t^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -3+5t^{2}+4t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-3+5t^{2}+4t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+5\\int t^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+4\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{t^{3}}{3}\\right)+4\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -3t+\\frac{5t^{3}}{3}+2t^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671338 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int 4-t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$." "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int 4-t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$."] [:db/cas 17592186671338 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 4-t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(4-t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{t^{7}}{7}\\right)+C\\\\ &= 4t-\\frac{t^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank." "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 4-t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(4-t^{6}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-\\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 4\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{t^{7}}{7}\\right)+C\\\\ &= 4t-\\frac{t^{7}}{7}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."] [:db/cas 17592186671339 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int -\\sqrt[4]{y}-y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $y>0$." "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int -\\sqrt[4]{y}-y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $y>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671339 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -\\sqrt[4]{y}-y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-y^{\\frac{1}{4}}-y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\int y^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y-\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{y^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{y^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{y^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank." "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -\\sqrt[4]{y}-y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-y^{\\frac{1}{4}}-y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\int y^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y-\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -\\left(\\frac{y^{\\frac{5}{4}}}{\\frac{5}{4}}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{4\\sqrt[4]{y^{5}}}{5}-\\frac{y^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."] [:db/cas 17592186671340 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 2t^{3}+3t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int 2t^{3}+3t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671340 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 2t^{3}+3t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(2t^{3}+3t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\int t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+3\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\left(\\frac{t^{4}}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{t^{4}}{2}+\\frac{3t^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 2t^{3}+3t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(2t^{3}+3t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\int t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+3\\int t^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 2\\left(\\frac{t^{4}}{4}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{t^{2}}{2}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{t^{4}}{2}+\\frac{3t^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671341 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Assume $x>0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int 1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$ Assume $x>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671341 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(1+\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}}{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= x-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(1+\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}}{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= x-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671342 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int t^{6}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$." "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int t^{6}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$."] [:db/cas 17592186671342 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int t^{6}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(t^{6}-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-2\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{t^{7}}{7}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{t^{7}}{7}-2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank." "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int t^{6}-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(t^{6}-2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int t^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-2\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\left(\\frac{t^{7}}{7}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{t^{7}}{7}-2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."] [:db/cas 17592186671343 :question/problem "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{-3t^{3}-6t}{t^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t\\ne0$." "Find the antiderivative:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{-3t^{3}-6t}{t^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t=\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C$. Assume $t\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671343 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{-3t^{3}-6t}{t^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-3-\\frac{6}{t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-6\\int t^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)-6\\left(\\frac{t^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -3t+\\frac{6}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank." "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{-3t^{3}-6t}{t^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-3-\\frac{6}{t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\int t^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-6\\int t^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= -3\\left(\\frac{t^{1}}{1}\\right)-6\\left(\\frac{t^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -3t+\\frac{6}{t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe constant $C$ is already supplied outside the blank."] [:db/cas 17592186671344 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 6y^{2}+5y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int 6y^{2}+5y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671344 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 6y^{2}+5y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(6y^{2}+5y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 6\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+5\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 6\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)+C\\\\ &= 2y^{3}+\\frac{5y^{4}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 6y^{2}+5y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(6y^{2}+5y^{3}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 6\\int y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+5\\int y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= 6\\left(\\frac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)+5\\left(\\frac{y^{4}}{4}\\right)+C\\\\ &= 2y^{3}+\\frac{5y^{4}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671345 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 5t^{5}-6\\sqrt[5]{t^{2}}+\\frac{3}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Assume $t\\ne0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int 5t^{5}-6\\sqrt[5]{t^{2}}+\\frac{3}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$ Assume $t\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671345 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 5t^{5}-6\\sqrt[5]{t^{2}}+\\frac{3}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(5t^{5}-6t^{\\frac{2}{5}}+\\frac{3}{t^{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 5\\int t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-6\\int t^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+3\\int t^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 5\\left(\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right)-6\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{7}{5}}}{\\frac{7}{5}}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{t^{-3}}{-3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{5t^{6}}{6}-\\frac{30\\sqrt[5]{t^{7}}}{7}-\\frac{1}{t^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int t^p\\,\\mathrm{d}t=t^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 5t^{5}-6\\sqrt[5]{t^{2}}+\\frac{3}{t^{4}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= \\int\\left(5t^{5}-6t^{\\frac{2}{5}}+\\frac{3}{t^{4}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 5\\int t^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t-6\\int t^{\\frac{2}{5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t+3\\int t^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\ &= 5\\left(\\frac{t^{6}}{6}\\right)-6\\left(\\frac{t^{\\frac{7}{5}}}{\\frac{7}{5}}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{t^{-3}}{-3}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{5t^{6}}{6}-\\frac{30\\sqrt[5]{t^{7}}}{7}-\\frac{1}{t^{3}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671346 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int -4+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y$. Assume $y>0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int -4+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$ Assume $y>0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671346 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -4+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-4+\\frac{1}{y^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -4\\int y^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\int y^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -4\\left(\\frac{y^{1}}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{y^{-\\frac{1}{2}}}{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -4y-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{y}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int y^p\\,\\mathrm{d}y=y^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int -4+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y^{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= \\int\\left(-4+\\frac{1}{y^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -4\\int y^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}y+\\int y^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\ &= -4\\left(\\frac{y^{1}}{1}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{y^{-\\frac{1}{2}}}{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -4y-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{y}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671347 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[7]{x^{8}}}-5x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x$. Assume $x\\ne0$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int \\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[7]{x^{8}}}-5x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$ Assume $x\\ne0$."] [:db/cas 17592186671347 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[7]{x^{8}}}-5x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(\\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\\frac{2}{x^{\\frac{8}{7}}}-5x^{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 3\\int x^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-2\\int x^{-\\frac{8}{7}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-5\\int x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 3\\left(\\frac{x^{-1}}{-1}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{x^{-\\frac{1}{7}}}{-\\frac{1}{7}}\\right)-5\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{14}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}-\\frac{5x^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int \\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[7]{x^{8}}}-5x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(\\frac{3}{x^{2}}-\\frac{2}{x^{\\frac{8}{7}}}-5x^{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 3\\int x^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-2\\int x^{-\\frac{8}{7}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x-5\\int x^{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 3\\left(\\frac{x^{-1}}{-1}\\right)-2\\left(\\frac{x^{-\\frac{1}{7}}}{-\\frac{1}{7}}\\right)-5\\left(\\frac{x^{6}}{6}\\right)+C\\\\ &= -\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{14}{\\sqrt[7]{x}}-\\frac{5x^{6}}{6}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671348 :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int 5x^{2}+2x+1\\,\\mathrm{d}x$." "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int 5x^{2}+2x+1\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671348 :question/worked-solution "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $\\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 5x^{2}+2x+1\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(5x^{2}+2x+1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 5\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+2\\int x^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 5\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{5x^{3}}{3}+x^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Rewrite as powers, apply the sum and constant factor rules, and integrate each term using $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x^p\\,\\mathrm{d}x=x^{p+1}/(p+1)+C\n$$ for $p\\ne-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}& \\int 5x^{2}+2x+1\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= \\int\\left(5x^{2}+2x+1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 5\\int x^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+2\\int x^{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\int x^{0}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\ &= 5\\left(\\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\right)+2\\left(\\frac{x^{2}}{2}\\right)+\\left(\\frac{x^{1}}{1}\\right)+C\\\\ &= \\frac{5x^{3}}{3}+x^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671882 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(y - 1 \\right)}$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\left(y-1\\right)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(y - 1 \\right)}$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\left(y-1\\right)<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186671896 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{x - 3}}{\\sqrt{y - 6}}.$" "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{x - 3}}{\\sqrt{y - 6}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186672117 :question/problem "**(b)** Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\nabla\\tan(xy)\\cdot d\\mathbf r$, where $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1/2,\\pi/2)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(b)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\nabla\\tan(xy)\\cdot d\\mathbf r,\n$$ where $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(1/2,\\pi/2)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672129 :question/problem "**(a)** Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ for\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\langle4xy+2z^2,2x^2,4xz\\rangle,$$\n\n$$C:\\ \\mathbf r(t)=\\langle3\\sin t,4\\sin t,5\\cos t\\rangle,\\qquad0\\le t\\le2\\pi.$$\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(a)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds\n$$ for\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\langle4xy+2z^2,2x^2,4xz\\rangle,$$\n\n$$C:\\ \\mathbf r(t)=\\langle3\\sin t,4\\sin t,5\\cos t\\rangle,\\qquad0\\le t\\le2\\pi.$$\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672196 :question/problem "**(c)** Show that $\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v)=2\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}$. Conclude that $\\displaystyle\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}=\\frac12\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" "**(c)** Show that $$\n\\displaystyle\\frac{d}{dt}(\\mathbf v\\cdot\\mathbf v)=2\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}.\n$$ Conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}=\\frac12\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2.\n$$\n\nYour response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186672200 :question/worked-solution "Separate and integrate: $\\int dR/(R^2+16)=ax+C_1$, so $\\frac14\\arctan(R/4)=ax+C_1$. Multiply by 4 and absorb 4C₁ into C, then take tangent. Each solution is restricted to an interval avoiding the poles of tan. Differentiation gives $R\\prime=16a\\sec^2(4ax+C)=a(R^2+16)$." "Separate and integrate: $$\n\\displaystyle \\int dR/(R^2+16)=ax+C_1,\n$$ so $\\frac14\\arctan(R/4)=ax+C_1$. Multiply by 4 and absorb 4C₁ into C, then take tangent. Each solution is restricted to an interval avoiding the poles of tan. Differentiation gives $R\\prime=16a\\sec^2(4ax+C)=a(R^2+16)$."] [:db/cas 17592186672221 :question/worked-solution "Integration by parts gives $\\int -xe^x dx=(1-x)e^x+C$. The condition at zero gives $C=1$. Differentiate to check $y\\prime=-xe^x$ and substitute zero to check $y(0)=2$." "Integration by parts gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\int -xe^x dx=(1-x)e^x+C.\n$$ The condition at zero gives $C=1$. Differentiate to check $y\\prime=-xe^x$ and substitute zero to check $y(0)=2$."] [:db/cas 17592186672232 :question/problem "**(d)** Use part (b) to show that $\\displaystyle-\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=m\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}$. Conclude from parts (a) and (c) that $\\displaystyle-\\frac{d\\phi}{dt}=\\frac m2\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" "**(d)** Use part (b) to show that $$\n\\displaystyle-\\nabla\\phi\\cdot\\mathbf r\\prime=m\\mathbf v\\cdot\\frac{d\\mathbf v}{dt}.\n$$ Conclude from parts (a) and (c) that $$\n\\displaystyle-\\frac{d\\phi}{dt}=\\frac m2\\frac{d}{dt}|\\mathbf v|^2.\n$$\n\nYour response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186672247 :question/problem "**(b)** Evaluate $\\int_{AB}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$. This equals $\\phi(B)-\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(b)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{AB}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$ This equals $\\phi(B)-\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672273 :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, $\\int2/x^3dx=-1/x^2$, so the integrating factor is $e^{-1/x^2}$. Then $(e^{-1/x^2}y)\\prime=3$, giving $y=e^{1/x^2}(3x+C)$ on an interval avoiding zero. The supplied key has a coefficient typo in one intermediate line; the original equation and its final answer agree." "For $x\\ne0$, $$\n\\displaystyle \\int2/x^3dx=-1/x^2,\n$$ so the integrating factor is $e^{-1/x^2}$. Then $(e^{-1/x^2}y)\\prime=3$, giving $y=e^{1/x^2}(3x+C)$ on an interval avoiding zero. The supplied key has a coefficient typo in one intermediate line; the original equation and its final answer agree."] [:db/cas 17592186672286 :question/problem "**(d)** $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" "**(d)** $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186672305 :question/problem "**(c)** Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint_C e^{-x}\\cos y\\,dx+e^{-x}\\sin y\\,dy$, where $C$ is the counterclockwise rectangle with vertices $(\\pm2,\\pm1)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(c)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C e^{-x}\\cos y\\,dx+e^{-x}\\sin y\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise rectangle with vertices $(\\pm2,\\pm1)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672348 :question/problem "**2.** For the same constants and initial population, find $\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}P(t)$: {{limit}}." "**2.** For the same constants and initial population, find $$\n\\displaystyle\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}P(t):\n$$ {{limit}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672360 :question/problem "Source question: Problem 2, PDF page 3.\n\nLet\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x^3}{x^2+1},\\sin^4 y,\\ln(z^2+1)\\right\\rangle,$$\n\nand $C:\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "Source question: Problem 2, PDF page 3.\n\nLet\n\n$$\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x^3}{x^2+1},\\sin^4 y,\\ln(z^2+1)\\right\\rangle,$$\n\nand $C:\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672360 :question/worked-solution "Every component depends only on its corresponding coordinate and is continuous for all real inputs. A global potential is $\\Phi=\\int_0^x u^3/(u^2+1)\\,du+\\int_0^y\\sin^4v\\,dv+\\int_0^z\\ln(w^2+1)\\,dw$. Differentiating these one-variable integrals gives $\\nabla\\Phi=\\mathbf F$. Since the curve begins and ends at $(0,1,0)$, the integral is zero. There is no need to evaluate the three antiderivatives explicitly." "Every component depends only on its corresponding coordinate and is continuous for all real inputs. A global potential is $$\n\\displaystyle \\Phi=\\int_0^x u^3/(u^2+1)\\,du+\\int_0^y\\sin^4v\\,dv+\\int_0^z\\ln(w^2+1)\\,dw.\n$$ Differentiating these one-variable integrals gives $\\nabla\\Phi=\\mathbf F$. Since the curve begins and ends at $(0,1,0)$, the integral is zero. There is no need to evaluate the three antiderivatives explicitly."] [:db/cas 17592186672372 :question/problem "**(c)** $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}" "**(c)** $$\n\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=0\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$ and the counterclockwise circle of radius $2$ centered at $(1,0)$.\n\nYour response: {{response}}"] [:db/cas 17592186672403 :question/problem "**(a)** Evaluate $\\int_{OA}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$. This equals $\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}." "**(a)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{OA}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.\n$$ This equals $\\phi(A)$.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."] [:db/cas 17592186672770 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $250L$ of water containing $6.25kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.025kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $250L$ of water containing $6.25kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.025kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{25}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{25})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672774 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $600L$ of water containing $49kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.035kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $12L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $18L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $600L$ of water containing $49kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.035kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $12L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $18L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{100}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{100})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672780 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $216L$ of water containing $2.7kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{18}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{18})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $216L$ of water containing $2.7kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.03kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{18}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{18})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672785 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $840L$ of water containing $33.6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{70}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{70})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $840L$ of water containing $33.6kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.04kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $24L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $36L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{70}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{70})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186672799 :question/problem "An aquarium initially holds $500L$ of water containing $34kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.02kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "An aquarium initially holds $500L$ of water containing $34kg$ of dissolved minerals.\nA solution containing $0.02kg/L$ of dissolved minerals is pumped into the aquarium at a rate of $20L/min$, while the completely mixed water is drained out at a rate of $30L/min$. If the amount of minerals $Q(t)$, in kilograms, in the tank $t$ minutes after pumping begins is given by\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q(t) = α(1 - \\frac{t}{50}) + β(1 - \\frac{t}{50})^{3}\n$$\nwhere $α$ and $β$ are constants, what is the value of $α + β$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186673413 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible\n\n$\\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nThe 3 pivot columns give $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\nBy the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n$$\n3+\\text{nullity}(A)=8,\\qquad \\text{nullity}(A)=8-3=5.\n$$\n\nHence $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$. Since rank is less than the number of columns, $A$ is not invertible and $\\det(A)=0$. Also, $\\text{Col}(A)$ has dimension 3. The number of pivot columns is 3, and the number of non-pivot columns is 5.\n\n- Statement I is false. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rank is less than the number of columns.\n\n- Statement III is false. $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only." "Since $A$ is a $8\\times 8$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{8} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=8=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nThe 3 pivot columns give $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\nBy the rank-nullity theorem,\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\n$$\n3+\\text{nullity}(A)=8,\\qquad \\text{nullity}(A)=8-3=5.\n$$\n\nHence $\\text{rank}(A)=3$ and $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$. Since rank is less than the number of columns, $A$ is not invertible and $\\det(A)=0$. Also, $\\text{Col}(A)$ has dimension 3. The number of pivot columns is 3, and the number of non-pivot columns is 5.\n\n- Statement I is false. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$.\n\n- Statement II is true. The rank is less than the number of columns.\n\n- Statement III is false. $\\text{nullity}(A)=5$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is II only."] [:db/cas 17592186674390 :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{25}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the entry $b_{25}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674400 :question/problem "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." "Find the entry $b_{22}$ of the matrix $B$, where\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674400 :question/worked-solution "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}=-9.$\n\n$B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$." "The entry $b_{22}$ lies at the intersection of the $2$nd row and $2$nd column. Therefore, $b_{22}=-9.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle B = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & \\sqrt{2} & 9 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -9 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 9 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 7 & 8 & -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674489 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 2 & \\boxed{4} & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 2 & \\boxed{4} & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674496 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & \\boxed{2} & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $Y$ given below. Describe it in terms of $y_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle Y = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 & -9 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 & 8 \\\\ -8 & \\boxed{2} & 4 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674498 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 7 & \\boxed{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 7 & \\boxed{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186674501 :question/problem "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -9 & \\boxed{7} & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" "Consider the highlighted entry of the matrix $P$ given below. Describe it in terms of $p_{ij}$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle P = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -9 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 5 & 